# A Go call in statement position aborts on a non-nil error, exactly as
# `v := f()` does. Whether a failure is noticed must not depend on
# whether the caller happened to want the value. This script pins the
# shapes that CONTINUE; the abort itself is in errors_test.go, where the
# reported position can be checked too.

# Naming the error is the opt-out, in both spellings.
_ = writeFile("no-such-dir/note.txt", "x")
fmt.Println("discarded explicitly")
err := writeFile("no-such-dir/note.txt", "x")
fmt.Println("named:", err != nil)

# A nil error is not an abort, and the results are still discarded.
writeFile("note.txt", "hello\n")
fmt.Println("wrote:", exists("note.txt"))

# A failing $(...) is data, not a failure: the status is reported through
# status(), and that holds in statement position too. This is the one
# call shape the rule exempts, and reaching it takes some doing -- a bare
# $(...) on its own line classifies as a SHELL line and never becomes a
# __capture call at all, so the capture has to sit inside a line the
# classifier has already called Go.
for i := 0; i < 1; i++ { $(false) }
fmt.Println("capture did not abort, status:", status())
